## Root Cause Investigation

Further troubleshooting revealed the affected user was connected through a CGNAT (Carrier-Grade NAT) network managed by their ISP.

Because the network infrastructure was shared across multiple users, the ISP restricted NAT modifications for security reasons.

### ISP Response

> "By permitting liberal NAT settings, we would risk the security of the entire network and its users and unfortunately, requires far too many ports to be opened for the consoles/games internet features to function correctly. Therefore, we cannot guarantee the efficacy of our firewall for your network. As such, at this time we do not support NAT-type changes for gaming consoles, and I will be closing your ticket."

---

## Why CGNAT Causes Problems

CGNAT (Carrier-Grade NAT) allows multiple users to share a single public IP address. While this helps conserve IPv4 addresses, it can create problems for peer-to-peer gaming connections.

### Common Issues
- Restricted inbound traffic
- Difficulty establishing peer-to-peer sessions
- Increased disconnects
- Inability to host multiplayer lobbies
- Strict NAT classifications (Type D/F)

Nintendo Switch online multiplayer relies heavily on peer-to-peer communication, making restrictive NAT environments particularly problematic.

